# Copy to `web/.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in ONE generator key.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE GENERATOR — this is what writes the answers
# ===========================================================================
#
# Without one of these, retrieval still works and still answers in under
# 200 ms, but the app can only show you the passage that matched, quoted and
# labelled as a quotation. The step that turns
#
#     name: priya rao, age: 31          ->    "You are 31."
#
# is the generator. It is the G in RAG. Pick exactly one of the three blocks
# below; the first one configured wins, in the order they appear here.
#
# None of these is `VITE_`-prefixed, and that is deliberate. Vite only inlines
# `VITE_*` into the browser bundle; these are read by the dev server in
# `vite.config.ts`, which runs in Node. The browser can call `/synthesize`, but
# it can never read what authenticates it.

# --- 1. Groq — free, and the fastest option there is ----------------------
#
# Default. ~1000 tokens/second on `openai/gpt-oss-20b`, which is quicker than
# any frontier model on any hosted GPU, and the free tier (30 requests/minute,
# no card) covers a demo and ordinary use. This step needs speed far more than
# it needs reasoning — the answer is already in the retrieved passage; the
# model's job is to say it as a sentence.
#
# Key: https://console.groq.com/keys
#
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...

# --- 2. Anthropic — paid, better at reasoning across passages -------------
#
# Worth it if answers ever need to combine facts from several passages rather
# than restate one. Defaults to claude-haiku-4-5; set LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# for the strongest option.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# --- 3. Anything else OpenAI-compatible, including local ------------------
#
# Cerebras, Together, OpenRouter, or a model running on your own machine.
# A local server needs no key at all — leave LLM_API_KEY unset — and keeps the
# app's "nothing is uploaded anywhere" claim literally true, at the cost of
# generating at your laptop's speed rather than a datacentre's.
#
#   Ollama:      LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
#                LLM_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b-instruct
#   llama.cpp:   LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/v1
#
# LLM_BASE_URL=
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=

# Force a provider when more than one key is present, or override the model
# for any of the three blocks above.
#
# LLM_PROVIDER=groq        # groq | anthropic | custom
# LLM_MODEL=

# ===========================================================================
# VITE_WORKER_BASE — the deployed Cloudflare Worker
# ===========================================================================
#
# Optional, and only for production. Leave it unset locally: with no value the
# app posts to its own origin, which the dev server answers using the key above.
#
# In production the Worker serves the same routes, plus three things the dev
# server cannot and a browser may not:
#
#   voice input     Sarvam authenticates with a custom HTTP header, which a
#                   browser cannot set on a WebSocket handshake
#   spoken answers  the ElevenLabs and Sarvam keys must not ship to the client
#   adding a link   a browser cannot read the body of a cross-origin page
#
# Its own secrets are set with `wrangler secret put`, never in this file.
#
# VITE_WORKER_BASE=https://chehrag-worker.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
